MASTER LIBRARY
To transition from a single list to a "Big Library" vs. "Small Library" structure, you are moving toward a multi-tenant or catalog-based architecture. This is the professional way to manage a "Master List" of wines while allowing individual restaurants to have their own unique menus.
The "State of the Art" way to do this in Supabase is to use a Relational Link rather than just an "active" column.

1. The Database Architecture (The "State of the Art" Way)
Instead of one big table where you mark things "active," you should have two tables:
Table A: master_wine_library (The Global Catalog)
Contains every wine possible (Producer, Region, Vintage, Grapes).
This is your "Single Source of Truth."
Table B: restaurant_inventory (The Local Menu)
Columns: restaurant_id, master_wine_id (Foreign Key), is_active (Boolean), custom_price, stock_level.
This allows two different restaurants to carry the same wine but at different prices or stock levels.

2. How to Adjust Your n8n Workflow
With this new structure, your workflow becomes more powerful because you no longer need to re-type the wine's name or region every time.
Step 1: The "Get Many" Move
You now need two "Get Many" steps:
Get Many from master_wine_library: To see if the wine exists globally.
Get Many from restaurant_inventory: To see if this specific restaurant already has it on their menu.
Step 2: The Logic Split
Your If Node now makes two decisions:
Decision 1: "Is this wine in the Master Library?"
If No: Use the Create command on the master_wine_library table first.
Decision 2: "Is this wine linked to this restaurant?"
If Yes: Use the Update command to change stock_level or set is_active = true.
If No: Use the Create command on the restaurant_inventory table to link the Master ID to the Restaurant ID.

3. Managing the "Active" State
Using an active column (Boolean: True/False) is excellent for performance. To make it "State of the Art":
The "Sync-to-Inactive" Logic: If a wine is missing from the incoming Toast file but exists in your Supabase restaurant_inventory, your workflow should use the Update command to set is_active = false instead of using the Deletecommand.
Why? Deleting removes history. Setting it to "Inactive" keeps your sales data intact while hiding the wine from the live menu.
The "Partial Update" Move: Use your Custom API Call to perform a "Soft Delete."
JSON Body: {"is_active": false, "stock_level": 0}.

